p>The Device Toolbar is near the top of the window.\n             The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Record to start recording.</cmd>\n           <info>\n             <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\n             A flat line means the microphone is not selected correctly: click Stop and check the recording device.</p>\n           </info>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Speak into your microphone, or play the audio you want to capture.</cmd>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Stop when you are finished.</cmd>\n         </step>\n         <step>\n           <cmd>Click Play to listen to your recording.</cmd>\n         </step>\n       </steps>\n       <result>\n         <p>You now have a recorded audio track in your project.\n         The waveform shows the shape of the recorded audio: louder sounds appear as taller waves.</p>\n       </result>\n     </taskbody>\n   </task>\n   ```\n2. **Read the task elements**\n\n   - `<task>` and `<taskbody>` take the place of `<concept>` and `<conbody>`. The DOCTYPE changes with them: `-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN`.\n   - `<context>` says why and when the reader does this task. It comes before the steps and holds ordinary paragraphs.\n   - `<steps>` is the procedure; each `<step>` is one action. The task body allows one `<steps>` (or `<steps-unordered>`, which stage 06 shows).\n   - `<cmd>` is the action itself, and is the one required child of a step. Write it as an imperative sentence. A reader who only reads the `<cmd>` elements should be able to complete the task.\n   - `<info>` is extra information about a step: what to expect, where to find the control. It is optional and sits after the `<cmd>`.\n   - `<result>` says what is true when the steps are done. It comes after the steps.\n:::\n\n## Step 2: Write the reference {#step-2-write-the-reference}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Create `topics/supported-audio-formats.dita`**\n\n   ```xml {title=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"}\n   <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n   <!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN\" \"reference.dtd\">\n\n   <reference id=\"supported-audio-formats\">\n     <title>Supported audio formats</title>\n     <shortdesc>The audio formats Audacity imports and exports, and when to use each one.</shortdesc>\n     <refbody>\n       <section>\n         <p>Audacity imports and exports audio in several formats.\n         Lossless formats keep every sample of the source; lossy formats discard detail to make smaller files.</p>\n       </section>\n       <table>\n         <title>Common audio formats</title>\n         <tgroup cols=\"4\">\n           <colspec colname=\"format\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"type\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"quality\" colwidth=\"1*\"/>\n           <colspec colname=\"notes\" colwidth=\"2*\"/>\n           <thead>\n             <row>\n               <entry>Format</entry>\n               <entry>Type</entry>\n               <entry>Quality</entry>\n               <entry>Notes</entry>\n             </row>\n           </thead>\n           <tbody>\n             <row>\n               <entry>WAV</entry>\n               <entry>Uncompressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossless</entry>\n               <entry>Large files, no quality loss.\n               Best for archiving and further editing.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>MP3</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossy</entry>\n               <entry>Widely supported, small files.\n               Good for sharing and the web.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>OGG Vorbis</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossy</entry>\n               <entry>Open alternative to MP3.\n               Good quality at low bitrates.</entry>\n             </row>\n             <row>\n               <entry>FLAC</entry>\n               <entry>Compressed</entry>\n               <entry>Lossless</entry>\n               <entry>Smaller than WAV with no quality loss.\n               Ideal for archiving when space matters.</entry>\n             </row>\n           </tbody>\n         </tgroup>\n       </table>\n     </refbody>\n   </reference>\n   ```\n2. **Read the reference elements**\n\n   - `<reference>` and `<refbody>`, with the DOCTYPE `-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN`. A reference body is made of sections, tables and property lists rather than free paragraphs, so the introductory `<p>` sits inside a `<section>`.\n   - `<table>` uses the CALS table model. Use it for row or column spanning, a table title, or detailed column specifications. Its optional `<title>` supplies the table caption. Simple tables also support headers and relative column widths, as stage 05 explains.\n   - `<tgroup cols=\"4\">` declares the number of columns and holds everything else. One table can have several `tgroup`s with different column layouts.\n   - `<colspec>` names each column and sets its width. `colwidth=\"2*\"` is a proportional width: this column gets twice the share of the `1*` columns.\n   - `<thead>` and `<tbody>` hold header and body rows; each `<row>` holds one `<entry>` per column.\n:::\n\n## Step 3: Update the README and run the gate {#step-3-update-the-readme-and-run-the-gate}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Change the \"You are on\" line**\n\n   ```diff {title=\"README.md\"}\n   --- a/README.md\n   +++ b/README.md\n   @@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n\n    Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n\n   -You are on stage 03: first build.\n   +You are on stage 04: task and reference.\n\n    ## Stages\n\n   ```\n2. **Format and check**\n\n   ```bash\n   dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita\n   scripts/check-stage.sh\n   ```\n\n   ```\n