      <h2 id="step-1-write-the-task" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: Write the task<a href="#step-1-write-the-task" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><code>topics/recording-your-first-track.dita</code>
      <CodeBlock code={"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE task PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN\" \"task.dtd\">\n\n<task id=\"recording-your-first-track\">\n  <title>Recording your first track</title>\n  <shortdesc>Make your first recording in Audacity: pick a microphone, record, stop and play it back.</shortdesc>\n  <taskbody>\n    <context>\n      <p>This task walks you through making your first audio recording.\n      You need a microphone connected to your computer.</p>\n    </context>\n    <steps>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Open Audacity.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <p>On first launch Audacity opens an empty project window with a gray waveform area.</p>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Select your microphone from the recording device list in the Device Toolbar.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <p>The Device Toolbar is near the top of the window.\n          The recording device list has a microphone icon next to it.</p>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Click Record to start recording.</cmd>\n        <info>\n          <p>A blue waveform appears as Audacity captures audio from your microphone.\n          A flat line means the microphone is not selected correctly: click Stop and check the recording device.</p>\n        </info>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Speak into your microphone, or play the audio you want to capture.</cmd>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Click Stop when you are finished.</cmd>\n      </step>\n      <step>\n        <cmd>Click Play to listen to your recording.</cmd>\n      </step>\n    </steps>\n    <result>\n      <p>You now have a recorded audio track in your project.\n      The waveform shows the shape of the recorded audio: louder sounds appear as taller waves.</p>\n    </result>\n  </taskbody>\n</task>"} lang="xml" title="topics/recording-your-first-track.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the task elements</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>&lt;task&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;taskbody&gt;</code> take the place of <code>&lt;concept&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;conbody&gt;</code>. The DOCTYPE changes with them: <code>-//OASIS//DTD DITA Task//EN</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;context&gt;</code> says why and when the reader does this task. It comes before the steps and holds ordinary paragraphs.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;steps&gt;</code> is the procedure; each <code>&lt;step&gt;</code> is one action. The task body allows one <code>&lt;steps&gt;</code> (or <code>&lt;steps-unordered&gt;</code>, which stage 06 shows).</li>
      <li><code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code> is the action itself, and is the one required child of a step. Write it as an imperative sentence. A reader who only reads the <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code> elements should be able to complete the task.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;info&gt;</code> is extra information about a step: what to expect, where to find the control. It is optional and sits after the <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;result&gt;</code> says what is true when the steps are done. It comes after the steps.</li>
      </ul>
